<h1>What is confirmed, and what is not</h1>
<p class="lede">This project runs on a division of labour: a panel argues, a chair synthesizes, and the author ratifies. Ratification is the one step performed by a person standing outside the machine. This page states, plainly, what the author has confirmed and what he has not — so no reader has to reconstruct it from the record.</p>

<p class="companion-note">Plain-language key. <strong>Ratified</strong> = the author, the one human check, has confirmed it. <strong>Provisional-pending-author</strong> = the panel and chair (which are, on the project's own admission, one underlying model in many roles) produced it under a delegation, and the author has not yet reviewed it; it changes counts and unblocks work, and the author can reverse any of it on return at no cost.</p>

<h2>The standing count</h2>
<p>As of Session 4j (13 July 2026): <strong>three consecutive standing delegations</strong> are open and stacked — the author asked the machine to keep working autonomously three sessions running (booked as costs C-027, C-032, C-035). The item-by-item review of what those sessions produced has been performed <strong>zero times</strong>. So the honest ratio is stark: the bulk of everything decided across Sessions 4h, 4i, and 4j is <strong>provisional-pending-author</strong>, not confirmed.</p>

